#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134975} :activity/id #uuid "66b245e8-8d46-5eb6-a9cb-721c5323145e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050810 :topic/id #uuid "6869b952-1056-567b-aee7-9b79143e8e5f" :topic/math-academy-id 2150} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133647 :question/id #uuid "e32fb82c-2e8f-5383-b15a-8f98aa86b716" :question/math-academy-id "e-6753"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329885}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "e944fcc1-fe2f-5c5d-9768-0e36d6d884b9" :step/math-academy-id 24962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134976}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135022 :tutorial/content "The **intermediate value theorem (IVT)** states that:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is continuous on $\\color{blue}[a,b],$ and $k$ is a number between $\\color{red}{f(a)}$ and $\\color{red}{f(b)}$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in \\color{blue}{[a,b]}$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nThe intuition behind the IVT is that if a function is continuous between two inputs, then between those two inputs, we must be able to draw the function without picking up our pen off the page, so the function must cover all values between the two corresponding outputs.\n\nThe IVT is often used to prove that some equation has a solution. For example, if we want to prove that there is a solution to $f(x)=3,$ and we know that $f(x)$ is a continuous function with $f({\\color{blue}{1}})={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $f({\\color{blue}{6}})={\\color{red}{6}},$ then the IVT tells us that there exists some solution $c \\in \\color{blue}{[1,6]}$ such that $f(c)=3.$\n\nWe can gain further intuition by visualizing the situation. First, let's plot what $y=f(x)$ *might* look like.\n\n![](images/98/9896ea1c3d3d7c461e78ce4b4b232ce1f13f329468cd28b2d8e743132de093df.png)\n\nFor this particular $f(x)$ there is one solution to $f(x)=3$, which is $x\\approx 2.$ Note that our solution lies inside the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$\n\nNow let's consider another *possible* $f(x).$\n\n![](images/de/dea5683d5b18bccf9623fb6a107e8ef818ddd1cef4adbef08be471869791ec27.png)\n\nThis time, there are three solutions to $f(x)=3$ which lie in the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$ \n\nWe can keep changing our $f(x),$ but no matter how we draw it, we will always have some solution to $f(x)=3$ which lies in the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$\n\n![](images/4d/4d4aaec84baff10a48f715eeae7e06af21beccd29b38c42b3283032ef0726fec.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9dc98e46-26fd-5ae1-b12b-05da48928cc5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 306 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b8f1b0d3-494f-537b-8e99-af2aca4fc4f6" :step/math-academy-id 564 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134752}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133955 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133477 :question/id #uuid "bb373a5d-aa71-58ed-82e3-b74a8f51d144" :question/math-academy-id "e-114"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398841}] :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"} :step/id #uuid "ce1aa6a3-c0f2-525f-a909-c241964d206f" :step/math-academy-id 565 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133956 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133478 :question/id #uuid "d048aae2-5773-5da7-ab18-35d41bfbb53a" :question/math-academy-id "e-115"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399489}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"} :step/id #uuid "804af12a-0f60-55f9-a36a-0d40e8fd270b" :step/math-academy-id 568 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135023 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+5, & 1\\le x<3 \\\\ -x+4, & 3\\le x<4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we apply the intermediate value theorem to $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,4]?$\n\nRemember, the intermediate value theorem assumes that the function is continuous. Here, the function $f(x)$ not continuous on $[1,4].$ So, we cannot apply the intermediate value theorem.\n\nIf we plot the function, we can get an idea of why the intermediate value theorem cannot be applied.\n\n![](images/9b/9b9543ec11b350cea4ca18e24a8db6ae8f72ffc38e64f973f9af2f83f1226400.png)\n\nThe graph shows that, for example, the equation $f(x)=1.5$ has no solution on $[1,4]$ despite the fact that $1.5$ lies between $f(1)=4$ and $f(4)=0.$\nSo, the intermediate value theorem does not hold.\n\n**Watch out!** The interval on which we apply the intermediate value theorem must *always* be closed. For example, we can't apply the theorem on an interval like $[1,3).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a6571cd4-d390-5e75-907d-726b500e6976" :tutorial/math-academy-id 309 :tutorial/title "Why Does the Function Have to Be Continuous?"} :step/id #uuid "ec27384a-d1f9-523f-b3ef-d8bed946e5e0" :step/math-academy-id 570 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134979}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133481 :question/id #uuid "89750383-d5a6-5bf4-b6b2-abd489e44d3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-116"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398834}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"} :step/id #uuid "79d4ad5d-f388-5fc5-a8b7-a72f14369138" :step/math-academy-id 571}] :activity/title "The Intermediate Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134019}]}
